1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,376
This is STAT 331, video 5 b. In video 5 a, we found the maximum likelihood estimator of sigma squared: the sum of squared residuals, divided by n.

2
00:00:13,826 --> 00:00:33,389
After this video, you can explain why that estimator is biased. You can use the unbiased estimator, which divides by n minus 2. You can read sigma hat from regression output. And you can prove that S squared is unbiased. This is Tutorial 1, questions 2 b and 3.

3
00:00:33,839 --> 00:00:44,740
The slides do not use the maximum likelihood estimator. They use sigma squared hat: the sum of e i squared, divided by n minus 2. Why?

4
00:00:45,190 --> 00:01:04,390
Here is our own simulation. We take the five x values 1 to 5. We generate y from a true line with sigma squared equal to 1. We repeat this 20,000 times. Each time, we calculate sigma squared hat M L. The histogram shows the 20,000 values.

5
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:18,515
The purple line is the true value, 1. The red line is the average of the estimates: 0.60. So the maximum likelihood estimator is too small on average. It is biased.

6
00:01:18,965 --> 00:01:33,130
Now divide by n minus 2, which is 3, instead of 5. The average of these estimates is 1.00. This estimator is unbiased: its expected value is sigma squared.

7
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:42,668
The slide gives the reason. We want to show that the expected value of the sum of e i squared over n minus 2 is sigma squared.

8
00:01:43,118 --> 00:01:56,515
The slide uses one fact. The sum of e i squared, divided by sigma squared, has a chi squared distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. The proof comes later in the course.

9
00:01:56,965 --> 00:02:15,632
The mean of a chi squared distribution is its degrees of freedom. The plot shows the chi squared density with 3 degrees of freedom, for n equals 5. Its mean is 3. The blue histogram is the sum of squared residuals from our simulation. It follows the curve.

10
00:02:16,082 --> 00:02:29,351
Now take the expectation. Take sigma squared outside. Then take 1 over n minus 2 outside. The expectation that remains is n minus 2. So the result is sigma squared.

11
00:02:29,801 --> 00:02:45,055
With the same fact, the expected value of sigma squared hat M L is n minus 2 over n, times sigma squared. For n equals 5, this is 0.6 sigma squared. This agrees with the simulation.

12
00:02:45,505 --> 00:02:56,427
This slide is optional. It is not on the slides. It gives a picture of the n minus 2. Take n equals 5. Suppose you know three of the residuals.

13
00:02:56,877 --> 00:03:07,779
The two constraints, sum of e i equals 0 and sum of x i e i equals 0, are two linear equations. They fix the last two residuals.

14
00:03:08,229 --> 00:03:22,309
So only n minus 2 residuals are free. We estimated two parameters, beta zero and beta one. Each one uses up one degree of freedom. This is why the denominator is n minus 2.

15
00:03:22,759 --> 00:03:35,580
The two estimators differ only in the denominator. Their ratio is n over n minus 2. For n equals 5, the ratio is 1.67. The difference is large.

16
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:46,419
For n equals 50, the ratio is only 1.04. This is why the slide says: often it does not matter when n is 50 or more.

17
00:03:46,869 --> 00:03:53,653
The brainhead data have n equals 236. The ratio is 1.009.

18
00:03:54,103 --> 00:04:06,946
For the brainhead data, sigma hat is 72.35 grams. The maximum likelihood version is 72.04 grams. The two values are almost equal.

19
00:04:07,396 --> 00:04:20,879
Here are all 236 people with the fitted line. Beta zero hat is 335.4323, and beta one hat is 0.2608.

20
00:04:21,329 --> 00:04:35,686
Sigma hat is the square root of the sum of squared residuals over n minus 2. It is 72.35 grams. The green band goes from sigma hat below the line to sigma hat above the line.

21
00:04:36,136 --> 00:04:44,947
On the right is a histogram of the 236 residuals. They are centred at 0. Their sum is exactly 0.

22
00:04:45,397 --> 00:05:02,591
The dashed lines are at minus and plus sigma hat. Sigma hat measures the typical size of a residual. In these data, 166 of the 236 residuals, about 70 percent, are inside these lines.

23
00:05:03,041 --> 00:05:30,583
Tutorial 1, question 2 b. The data are 100 Boeing 787 aircraft. y is the maintenance cost in thousands of dollars, and x is the number of flight hours. The question gives this output. Verify the estimate of sigma. Pause the video and try it. The residual standard error is the estimate sigma hat. The output gives 426, on 98 degrees of freedom.

24
00:05:31,033 --> 00:05:38,883
The question also gives the sum of squared residuals: 17,818,085.

25
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:55,248
Divide by n minus 2. n is 100, so n minus 2 is 98. This is the 98 degrees of freedom in the output. Sigma squared hat is 181,817.2.

26
00:05:55,698 --> 00:06:07,794
Take the square root. Sigma hat is 426.40, in thousands of dollars. Rounded, this is 426. It agrees with the output.

27
00:06:08,244 --> 00:06:20,703
If you divide by n, you get the maximum likelihood value, 422.11. That is not the value in the output. The output uses n minus 2.

28
00:06:21,153 --> 00:06:45,217
Tutorial 1, question 3. Show that S squared, the sum of squared residuals over n minus 2, is an unbiased estimator of sigma squared. Pause the video and try it. The plan: show that the expected sum of squared residuals is n minus 2 times sigma squared. We do not use the chi squared fact. We use only means and variances.

29
00:06:45,667 --> 00:06:54,435
First, write the fitted value in a new form. Put in beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar.

30
00:06:54,885 --> 00:07:12,634
Collect the terms. y i hat is y bar plus beta one hat times x i minus x bar. In the picture, the fitted line goes through the point x bar, y bar. The green part is beta one hat times x i minus x bar.

31
00:07:13,084 --> 00:07:28,295
So the residual is y i minus y bar, minus beta one hat times x i minus x bar. In the picture, the blue distance y i minus y bar is the green part plus the orange residual.

32
00:07:28,745 --> 00:07:41,865
Square the residual and add over i. Expand the square of a difference. We get S y y, minus 2 beta one hat S x y, plus beta one hat squared S x x.

33
00:07:42,315 --> 00:07:57,269
Beta one hat is S x y over S x x. So S x y equals beta one hat times S x x. Put this into the middle term. It becomes minus 2 beta one hat squared S x x.

34
00:07:57,719 --> 00:08:25,730
So the sum of squared residuals is S y y minus beta one hat squared S x x. The bars check this with the brainhead data. S y y is 3,309,754. Beta one hat squared S x x is 2,084,853. The difference, 1,224,901, is the sum of squared residuals.

35
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:41,028
Next, find the expected value of S y y. Write y i with the model. Its mean y bar is beta zero plus beta one x bar plus epsilon bar. Subtract. Beta zero cancels.

36
00:08:41,478 --> 00:08:45,510
Square and add over i. S y y has three terms.

37
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:54,472
The first term, beta one squared S x x, is a constant, because the x i are fixed. Its expectation is itself.

38
00:08:54,922 --> 00:09:06,634
The middle term is a sum of fixed numbers times epsilon i minus epsilon bar. Each epsilon has mean 0. By linearity, its expectation is 0.

39
00:09:07,084 --> 00:09:26,028
The last term is the sum of epsilon i minus epsilon bar, squared. Its expectation is n minus 1 times sigma squared. The next slide shows why. So the expected S y y is beta one squared S x x plus n minus 1 sigma squared.

40
00:09:26,478 --> 00:09:39,491
Expand the square. The middle term has the sum of epsilon i, which is n epsilon bar. So the sum is the sum of epsilon i squared, minus n epsilon bar squared.

41
00:09:39,941 --> 00:09:55,579
Each epsilon i has mean 0 and variance sigma squared. So the expected epsilon i squared is sigma squared. The n terms give n sigma squared. These are the five orange blocks, for n equals 5.

42
00:09:56,029 --> 00:10:17,255
Epsilon bar also has mean 0. The epsilons are independent, so the variance of their sum is n sigma squared. Divide by n squared: the variance of epsilon bar is sigma squared over n. So n epsilon bar squared has expectation sigma squared. This removes one block.

43
00:10:17,705 --> 00:10:27,668
So the expectation is n minus 1 times sigma squared. This is the same reason that the sample variance divides by n minus 1.

44
00:10:28,118 --> 00:10:47,809
Now the second part, the expected value of beta one hat squared times S x x. First write beta one hat as a linear function of the y i. The sum of x i minus x bar, times y bar, is 0. So we can drop y bar. This step is on the slides.

45
00:10:48,259 --> 00:11:08,419
Take the expectation. The expected y i is beta zero plus beta one x i. The sum of x i minus x bar times beta zero is 0. The sum of x i minus x bar times x i is S x x. So the expected beta one hat is beta one.

46
00:11:08,869 --> 00:11:13,541
So the distribution of beta one hat has its centre at beta one.

47
00:11:13,991 --> 00:11:29,543
The y i are independent, each with variance sigma squared. The variance of a sum of constants times y i is the sum of the squared constants times sigma squared. This gives sigma squared over S x x.

48
00:11:29,993 --> 00:11:45,502
For any random variable W, the expected W squared is its variance plus its mean squared. So the expected beta one hat squared, times S x x, is sigma squared plus beta one squared S x x.

49
00:11:45,952 --> 00:12:06,603
Now combine the two results. The expected sum of squared residuals is the expected S y y, minus the expected beta one hat squared S x x. The top row is the expected S y y: beta one squared S x x, plus n minus 1 blocks of sigma squared.

50
00:12:07,053 --> 00:12:13,474
The second row is what we subtract: beta one squared S x x, plus one sigma squared.

51
00:12:13,924 --> 00:12:20,303
The beta one squared S x x terms cancel. One block of sigma squared also cancels.

52
00:12:20,753 --> 00:12:30,438
What remains is n minus 2 blocks of sigma squared. So the expected sum of squared residuals is n minus 2 times sigma squared.

53
00:12:30,888 --> 00:12:48,787
Divide by n minus 2. The expected S squared is sigma squared. So S squared is an unbiased estimator of sigma squared. Note that we did not use normality here. We used only the means, the variances, and the independence of the errors.

54
00:12:49,237 --> 00:13:01,951
To recap. The maximum likelihood estimator of sigma squared divides by n. It is too small on average. The estimator that divides by n minus 2 is unbiased.

55
00:13:02,401 --> 00:13:13,601
For 50 or more observations, the difference is small. In regression output, the residual standard error is sigma hat, with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

56
00:13:14,051 --> 00:13:34,318
To prove unbiasedness, write the sum of squared residuals as S y y minus beta one hat squared S x x. Take the expectation of each part. The result is n minus 2 times sigma squared. The next video covers the mean, variance, and distribution of beta one hat.
